1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:03,880
June, 2026, Bolzano, Italy.

2
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:06,980
Behind a steel door kept at minus six degrees,

3
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:09,600
scientists finish a test they have been running for years.

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:12,600
The result sounds impossible.

5
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,780
On the body of a man who has been dead for 5,300 years,

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:22,460
something is alive. Four species of yeast growing in the dark on

7
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:23,940
the world's most famous corpse.

8
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:28,260
Three of them have learned to eat the disinfectant that was meant to protect him.

9
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:31,400
And the man they live on is a murder victim,

10
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:36,360
the oldest one on Earth, shot in the back and left on the ice with everything he owned.

11
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:40,840
For 30 years, science was sure it knew how he died.

12
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,500
Last November, that answer fell apart.

13
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:45,200
This is the whole case file.

14
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:47,880
You're watching mindwired.

15
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,040
This is the story of the Iceman.

16
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:53,280
The murder, the evidence,

17
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:56,860
and the parts of the case that refuse to stay closed.

18
00:00:58,260 --> 00:00:59,960
Chapter one, the shortcut.

19
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:07,040
September 19th, 1991. 3,200 meters up in the Tyrolian Alps,

20
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:10,200
a German couple from Nuremberg leaves the marked path,

21
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:11,880
looking for a faster way down.

22
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:14,600
Erika Simon sees it first.

23
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,660
The back of her head, shoulders,

24
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:19,460
and a spine, sticking out of the snow and ice.

25
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,200
She and her husband, Helmut,

26
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:22,660
assume it is a dead climber.

27
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:25,460
By the couple's own account,

28
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:28,700
Helmut's camera had exactly one frame left on the roll.

29
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:30,900
He spent it on a single photograph,

30
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:35,660
the first picture ever taken of what would become the most studied human being in history.

31
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:39,560
They report the find at the nearest mountain refuge.

32
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:45,080
The keeper calls the police in two countries because nobody can say which side of the

33
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:46,700
border the dead man is lying on.

34
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:51,060
The next day, a jaundarm attacks the ice with a pneumatic drill.

35
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:54,400
It tears into the body's hip before the compressed air runs out.

36
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:57,960
The weather closes in. All they bring down is a strange axe.

37
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:01,700
That axe bothers people. It looks old,

38
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:04,260
genuinely old. And because of it,

39
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,420
the authorities open a criminal file on the body and the ice.

40
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:12,400
Over the next two days, eight separate groups climb up to see the body.

41
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:16,000
Souvenirs are taken. Fragile objects are stepped on.

42
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,620
The crime scene, and it will turn out to be one,

43
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:21,500
is contaminated before anyone knows it matters.

44
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,040
On September 23rd, a forensic doctor frees the corpse with an ice pick and a ski

45
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:31,020
pole, lifts it with his bare hands into a body bag,

46
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:33,140
and a helicopter carries it off the mountain.

47
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:39,180
One day later, an archaeologist takes a single look at the axe and the leathery skin

48
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:41,560
and says the words that change everything.

49
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:44,040
At least 4,000 years old.

50
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:50,440
A border survey follows. The body had lain 92 .5 metres inside Italy under

51
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,280
a line that had been drawn wrong because in 1919,

52
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:56,020
the true watershed was hidden beneath the ice.

53
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:59,620
Then radiocarbon dating lands like a hammer.

54
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:03,060
The man is roughly 5,300 years old,

55
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:06,240
the oldest natural human mummy ever found in Europe.

56
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:10,000
Chapter 2, The Man in the Ice.

57
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:13,300
He was about 45 years old,

58
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:17,140
one metre 60 tall, around 50 kilos in life.

59
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:18,880
In the museum's reconstruction,

60
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:20,500
he looks back at you like a neighbour.

61
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:25,380
But hold that thought, because every detail of that face is an educated guess.

62
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:29,240
His gear was real, and it was startling.

63
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:34,380
A copper -bladed axe, the only completely preserved prehistoric axe on Earth.

64
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:37,960
The blade is 99 .7 % pure copper.

65
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:41,180
And the copper did not come from the Alps.

66
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:44,560
Isotope analysis traced the ore to southern Tuscany,

67
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:49,040
500 kilometres away. Trade networks were moving metal across Europe,

68
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:50,840
5,000 years before highways.

69
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:56,340
He also carried an unfinished longbow and a quiver holding 14 arrows.

70
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:58,400
Only two of them were finished.

71
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:00,460
Whatever he was preparing for,

72
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:02,100
he died in the middle of it,

73
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:07,240
under -armed. His clothing came from at least five different animals.

74
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:12,240
Shimp, goat, cattle, brown bear for the cap,

75
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:16,600
roe deer for the quiver. What you can visit today are reconstructions,

76
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:18,060
and they fill a museum room.

77
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:21,440
His body kept a diary of his pain,

78
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:24,580
61 tattoos, in 19 groups,

79
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:28,580
most of them sitting directly over worn joints and his lower spine.

80
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:30,580
Researchers believed they were treatment,

81
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:35,540
not decoration. He was a walking case file even before the violence.

82
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:37,340
Whipped worm eggs in his gut,

83
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:41,260
hardening arteries, teeth ground down by gritty flower.

84
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:45,400
His DNA even carries traces of the bacterium that causes Lyme disease.

85
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:51,500
And a surviving fingernail recorded three separate episodes of serious illness in his

86
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,680
final six months. His body was already fighting something when he climbed for the last

87
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:58,560
time. None of that killed him.

88
00:04:59,220 --> 00:05:02,980
What killed him stayed hidden inside his left shoulder for 10 more years.

89
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:04,540
Through every examination,

90
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:09,400
every scan, every documentary that called his death an accident of weather.

91
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:15,100
Chapter 3, The Arrowhead June 2001,

92
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:17,040
Bolzano General Hospital.

93
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:22,440
A radiologist named Paul Gosner is reviewing new chest x -rays of the mummy taken to

94
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:23,900
check a suspected broken rib.

95
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:28,200
In the left shoulder, he spots a dense little triangle,

96
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:30,520
five times the density of bone.

97
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:32,780
On a body from the copper age,

98
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:35,200
only one material reads like that,

99
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:40,660
stone. It is a flint arrowhead about two and a half centimeters long

100
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:42,740
buried under the left shoulder blade,

101
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:48,620
10 years of the most intense scientific attention on Earth and everyone had missed the

102
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:53,000
murder weapon. It had been missed because the wooden shaft was gone.

103
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:54,960
Someone pulled the arrow out,

104
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:57,040
the dying man reaching back,

105
00:05:57,340 --> 00:05:59,600
or his killer removing evidence.

106
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:02,100
Only the stone tip stayed behind.

107
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:07,880
The pathologist melted the icy glaze on the mummy's back and found the entry wound,

108
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:11,560
small and unmistakable, below the left shoulder blade.

109
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:16,700
On July 26, 2001, the team announced it to the world.

110
00:06:17,380 --> 00:06:22,000
Europe's oldest mummy became the world's oldest identified murder victim and the very

111
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,300
first estimate said he could have lived only a few hours after the shot.

112
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:28,660
Remember that estimate. Chapter four,

113
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:32,360
a matter of minutes. Four years later,

114
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:34,780
the mummy went through a modern CT scanner,

115
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,360
slice by slice. What came back looked like case closed.

116
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:42,880
The arrow had torn the wall of the left subclavian artery,

117
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:48,760
a 13 millimeter tear in one of the body's critical vessels with a large bleed pooled

118
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:52,820
around it. The verdict, published in 2007,

119
00:06:53,220 --> 00:06:56,080
was brutal and simple, complete bleed out.

120
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:58,900
In the words of one researcher on the team,

121
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:00,560
if the artery starts bleeding,

122
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:02,640
it's a matter of minutes to die.

123
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:06,520
For nearly 20 years, that was the answer.

124
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:09,520
Museums printed it. Documentaries repeated it.

125
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:12,160
The ice man was shot in the back and died fast,

126
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:16,320
right where he fell. But the body held complications,

127
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:19,740
a deep cut across his right hand down to the bone.

128
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:23,200
Wound healing dated it three to eight days before death.

129
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:26,400
He had been in a fight, days before someone shot him.

130
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:30,400
A follow -up study concluded he was attacked at least twice.

131
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:33,080
The hand wound in one violent event,

132
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,700
the arrow and a blunt trauma to the back in another.

133
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:41,060
Later, researchers even reported possible bruising on his brain.

134
00:07:42,740 --> 00:07:44,600
Chapter five, the coldest case.

135
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:49,140
In 2016, the museum's director did something unusual.

136
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:51,300
She invited a working murder detective,

137
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:53,280
Alexander Horn of the Munich Police,

138
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:56,560
to profile a killing 5,300 years old.

139
00:07:58,060 --> 00:07:59,640
Horn started where detectives start,

140
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:01,500
the victim's final hours.

141
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:04,180
Pollen in his gut had already mapped them,

142
00:08:04,740 --> 00:08:06,420
down from the timber line into the valley,

143
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:10,220
then back up hard to over 3,000 metres.

144
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:14,940
Roughly 33 hours, reconstructed almost step by step.

145
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:18,440
Then the detail that anchors everything.

146
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:21,640
His stomach was full. Dried ibex,

147
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:24,460
meat and fat, red deer, einkorn wheat,

148
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:27,880
a heavy mountain meal, roughly half fat,

149
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:29,600
eaten shortly before he died.

150
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:35,880
In Horn's words, a man rushing to get away from someone trying to kill him does not sit

151
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:37,260
down and eat a heavy meal.

152
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:40,640
The iceman felt safe. He thought it was over.

153
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:44,240
Nothing was stolen. The copper axe,

154
00:08:44,740 --> 00:08:47,900
rare, valuable, instantly recognisable,

155
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:49,440
was still lying with him.

156
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:52,160
To Horn, that ruled out robbery,

157
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:54,360
and it whispered something darker.

158
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:58,180
The killer did not dare carry an object everyone back home would recognise.

159
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:03,440
Horn's read of the file. The hand wound was a fight the iceman won,

160
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:05,300
days earlier, down in the valley.

161
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:07,320
The arrow was the loser's answer,

162
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:11,120
fired from behind, from a distance he put at nearly 100 feet,

163
00:09:11,540 --> 00:09:15,980
while the victim rested. Other researchers read politics instead.

164
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:20,060
One archaeologist argued the iceman was a leader whose time had run out,

165
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:24,000
chased from his own village by rivals and finished off at altitude.

166
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:28,980
One scientist even reported blood from four different people on his gear.

167
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:31,720
On the knife. On an arrow.

168
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:35,800
On his coat. He died before the work was fully published,

169
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:38,300
and it has never been independently verified.

170
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:41,500
Every theory agrees on one thing.

171
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,920
Whoever released that arrow walked away clean.

172
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:46,040
No weapon. No second body.

173
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:50,760
No trace. The killer left exactly one piece of physical evidence on this earth,

174
00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:52,680
and it was inside his victim's shoulder.

175
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:56,600
Chapter 6. Hours, not minutes.

176
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:03,460
May 2025. A forensic team in Copenhagen rebuilds the wound in three dimensions from

177
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:06,560
the CT data, and the numbers start to disagree with the story.

178
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:09,400
The arrow had punched a hole,

179
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:10,740
cleaned through the shoulder blade,

180
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:12,960
and came to rest between the ribs and the scapula,

181
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:15,260
but the blood pooled around the wound,

182
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:17,880
measured only about 110 milliliters.

183
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:23,620
A tenth of a litre. That is not what bleeding to death in minutes looks like.

184
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:25,540
The team wrote it carefully.

185
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:29,120
Death from blood loss alone may not have been immediate.

186
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:32,260
The trajectory surprised them too.

187
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:33,740
The shot came from behind,

188
00:10:34,140 --> 00:10:35,380
angled slightly downward,

189
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:39,880
which means the killer may have held the high ground while the iceman stood below him,

190
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:43,940
unaware. Then, November 27th,

191
00:10:44,020 --> 00:10:49,220
2025. A clinical team with a practising neurosurgeon on it corrects the wound

192
00:10:49,220 --> 00:10:51,960
for 5,000 years of mummification shrinkage.

193
00:10:52,460 --> 00:10:56,600
In a living body, that wound track runs 11 to 13 centimetres,

194
00:10:57,040 --> 00:10:58,620
almost all of it through muscle.

195
00:11:00,140 --> 00:11:02,040
And muscle closes over a wound track.

196
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:06,920
Their estimate, about half a litre of blood lost before the bleeding stopped itself.

197
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:09,300
10 % of his blood volume,

198
00:11:09,900 --> 00:11:11,600
survivable. In their words,

199
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:14,980
survival for several hours after the arrow injury was likely.

200
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:19,680
So what killed him? Their answer is quieter and crueler.

201
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:21,720
Everything at once. The wound,

202
00:11:22,060 --> 00:11:24,380
plus hours of exertion, plus altitude,

203
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:27,280
plus cold. A slow fade on the ice,

204
00:11:27,540 --> 00:11:28,760
not a switch being flipped.

205
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:30,600
And his rigid right hand,

206
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:32,400
they suggest, was not a fist.

207
00:11:32,860 --> 00:11:36,460
It was a bandaged wound. Notice the circle.

208
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:39,480
The very first estimate, in 2001,

209
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:42,840
said hours. 2007 said minutes,

210
00:11:43,180 --> 00:11:45,280
and the world believed it for two decades.

211
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:48,140
24 years and two reversals later,

212
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:50,760
the science came home. Hours.

213
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:53,300
He sat on that ice, wounded,

214
00:11:53,820 --> 00:11:56,880
and he waited. Chapter seven,

215
00:11:57,260 --> 00:12:02,460
still alive. Which brings us back to Bolzano and the study that gave this video

216
00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:07,720
its title. Since 1998, the Iceman has lain in a sealed cell

217
00:12:07,720 --> 00:12:10,580
at minus six degrees and 99 % humidity,

218
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:13,000
resting on a scale that weighs him around the clock.

219
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:14,660
The cell is opened rarely.

220
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:16,440
Every opening is recorded.

221
00:12:17,640 --> 00:12:20,880
The new study reached across that entire recorded history.

222
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:24,280
Soil bagged at the gully during the 1991 recovery.

223
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:30,680
Stomach contents extracted in 2010 during a nine -hour thaw that yielded 149 samples.

224
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:34,220
Swabs and meltwater from a five -hour thaw in 2019.

225
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:39,480
Standard incubators could not get cold enough for what the team suspected was living

226
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:42,940
there. So they had a culturing device custom built to run cold,

227
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:47,200
and they waited. Four species of yeast grew.

228
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:49,420
Cold -adapted glacier organisms,

229
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:53,220
whose closest known relatives live in Antarctica and the high Arctic.

230
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:55,060
This was not lab contamination.

231
00:12:55,600 --> 00:12:57,140
It came with him out of the ice.

232
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:00,620
Three of the four can digest phenol.

233
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:05,840
That is the disinfectant sprayed on the mummy in 1991 to stop fungal growth.

234
00:13:06,280 --> 00:13:10,160
The chemical meant to protect him may have been quietly feeding what lives on him.

235
00:13:11,580 --> 00:13:12,840
And then the quiet bombshell.

236
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:18,480
One yeast, glacial xyma, was more abundant in the 2019 samples than in the 2010

237
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:20,860
samples, with less damage to its DNA.

238
00:13:21,560 --> 00:13:24,400
Between two openings of a sealed subzero cell,

239
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:28,580
it had been multiplying. Be precise about what this means.

240
00:13:29,180 --> 00:13:30,760
Nothing of the man himself is alive.

241
00:13:30,760 --> 00:13:34,400
His own cells died on that ridge 5,000 years ago.

242
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:38,760
His original gut bacteria survived only as shredded fragments of ancient DNA.

243
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:41,380
What lives is what the ice gave him.

244
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:47,100
Passengers still riding. The senior scientist on the study put it this way.

245
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:49,420
The mummy is not a static relic,

246
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:51,800
but a dynamic biological system.

247
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:56,300
Keeping the world's most protected corpse means monitoring the things that live on it

248
00:13:56,300 --> 00:14:00,740
forever. And in the strangest coder in this entire file,

249
00:14:00,740 --> 00:14:04,720
the team coaxed yeast recovered from his gut into fermenting sourdough.

250
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:06,400
The lead author's verdict,

251
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:09,600
quote, we made some really good dough with it.

252
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:12,420
Chapter eight, who he was all along.

253
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:15,260
Because here is the unsettling part.

254
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:17,200
Even his face has been overturned.

255
00:14:17,700 --> 00:14:18,900
The famous museum figure,

256
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:21,100
pale skin, a full head of hair,

257
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:23,980
wiry beard, was built on his first genome,

258
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:28,180
sequenced in 2012. In 2023,

259
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:32,760
a cleaner genome showed that his first sequence had been contaminated with modern DNA.

260
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:35,220
The real Iceman had dark skin,

261
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:38,260
the darkest recorded for any European of his time.

262
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:41,160
Dark eyes and a balding head.

263
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:46,500
More than 92 % of his ancestry traces to early farmers from Anatolia.

264
00:14:47,260 --> 00:14:50,360
And when researchers sequenced 47 of his prehistoric neighbors,

265
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:52,840
they found his community shared that ancestry.

266
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:55,280
Genetically, he fit right in.

267
00:14:56,440 --> 00:15:01,800
Except for two threads. His father's genetic line matches none of the 47 neighbors sampled,

268
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:05,340
and his mother's line has never been found in any population on Earth,

269
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:07,860
ancient or modern. Both threads,

270
00:15:08,120 --> 00:15:11,740
so far, end with him. The tabloids,

271
00:15:12,020 --> 00:15:13,600
meanwhile, gave him a curse.

272
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:16,720
Seven deaths among people who touched the case.

273
00:15:17,340 --> 00:15:21,480
The archaeologist who led the first investigation called it a load of rubbish and media

274
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:25,480
hype, and he was right. Hundreds of people have handled the Iceman.

275
00:15:26,020 --> 00:15:31,060
Time passes. People die. But one story from that list deserves

276
00:15:31,060 --> 00:15:33,400
better than a tabloid. Helmut Simon,

277
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:38,680
who found the Iceman, died in a mountain blizzard in 2004 on a different peak,

278
00:15:39,120 --> 00:15:43,480
200 kilometers away. He was missing for eight days before researchers found him in the

279
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:46,840
snow. He never lived to see the settlement his family had fought for.

280
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:49,440
Here is what stays with me.

281
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:51,660
We aimed every machine we have at this man.

282
00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:54,460
CT scanners. Gene sequences.

283
00:15:55,140 --> 00:15:59,520
Custom cold incubators. And he answered almost every question,

284
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:02,000
except the one a neighbor could have answered in a sentence.

285
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:07,860
Who wanted him dead? The killer's name melted away 5,000 years before anyone thought

286
00:16:07,860 --> 00:16:11,420
to ask. The arrowhead in his shoulder is the only testimony left,

287
00:16:11,660 --> 00:16:13,820
and it will never say more than it already has.

288
00:16:15,180 --> 00:16:18,320
And the man himself lies in the dark at minus six degrees.

289
00:16:19,240 --> 00:16:21,440
Still teaching. Still being corrected.

290
00:16:22,020 --> 00:16:23,980
While the only living things he has left,

291
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:25,700
the ones the Ice gave him,

292
00:16:25,860 --> 00:16:27,640
quietly multiplying the cold.

293
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:31,400
If the oldest cold case on Earth got under your skin,

294
00:16:31,700 --> 00:16:36,100
wait until you see the body still on Mount Everest and the man the whole world called

295
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:37,920
by the wrong name for 30 years.

296
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:41,360
That full story is on your screen right now.

